ge's tests also reformatted `delivery_surface_test.go` (formatting only), declared among the artifacts.\n - 2026-11-26: review round 9 (rrn-c3e24e7943c0693e, Claude Sonnet 5.5) requested changes. N1: the claim on `.pose/policy/delegation.json` is withdrawn: the reviewer profile is an operational change of this checkout, committed without the trailer, not a deliverable of the spec. N2: an entry is one Markdown list item: indented lines (also after a blank line) continue it, an unindented line or the next `-`/`*` item ends it, a wrapped `#` is no heading, and a nested list or a limit without its reason is reported and not sealed. N3: an ADR that links the spec's dated file cites it. N4 (fences close only with their own marker) and N5 (the governance text prints `within_limit`) are fixed in part; the rest are follow-ups. N6: the conclusion step of `review-brief/11` (re-recorded digest) says a follow-up with a sealed limit is recorded within it. N7 was recorded within L1. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n+- 2026-11-26: review round 10 (rrn-87881c122439e5f3) requested changes: a lazy unindented continuation and a fence inside an item still cut a limit. The section is now read fail-closed: it holds only limits, each one list item (`-`, `*` or `+`, indented up to three spaces) whose wrapped lines are indented under its marker; any other line (prose, a lazy continuation, a nested list, a fence, an ordered item) is reported, and the limit it belongs to is not sealed, so a limit is never sealed in part. Inline triple backticks no longer open a fence, an indented heading is read, an unterminated HTML comment is reported (N2, N4 in part), and the CLI reference states `within-limit` (N3). Each rule has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n \n \n ### Results summary\n@@ -176,7 +178,7 @@ L1 and L2 above.\n \n \n ### Follow-ups\n-- [open] Make the accepted-limits reader refuse the Markdown it still misreads: an indented code example, a line opening with inline backticks, an unterminated `<!--` that hides later limits, and an HTML comment written inside backticks in a limit's text (review round 9, N4). (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-12-26)\n+- [open] Keep an HTML comment written inside inline code in an accepted limit's sealed text, which the comment stripping now removes (review rounds 9 N4 and 10 N4). (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-12-26)\n - [open] Show within-limit findings in the text output of `pose review-check` and `pose closeout-check`, as the JSON already does (review round 9, N5). (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-12-26)\n \n \ndiff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 57f95eef..a8bb2a8d 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -1075,7 +1075,7 @@ Attach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `po\n \n **Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n \n-**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit, which may wrap over several lines (fenced blocks and HTML comments are not read as declarations); `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity and is counted apart in the governance outcomes; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\n+**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit, whose wrapped lines are indented under it; the section holds only limits, and any other line in it is reported and leaves the limit it belongs to unsealed (HTML comments are not read); `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity and is counted apart in the governance outcomes; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\n \n **Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux the dispatch runs the adapter under a review run shim, the same executable re-entered, which is the subreaper of the run's subtree: after the adapter exits, on a timeout, and when its dispatcher ends, it ends and reaps every process left in that subtree, even one that started its own session and cleared its environment, and reports the ended pids through a pipe the reviewer does not inherit; the dispatch lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory. An embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to ending the processes whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); on other Unix platforms only the process group is ended, and on Windows the adapter runs in a kill-on-close job object that the dispatch terminates after the run, on a timeout and, through the job's limit, when the dispatcher ends. The brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, under a lock the system releases with its holder, and a release completes with any run the attempt already recorded instead of writing another. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\n \ndiff --git a/docs-site/docs/cli.md b/docs-site/docs/cli.md\nindex 1611698b..212c45f8 100644\n--- a/docs-site/docs/cli.md\n+++ b/docs-site/docs/cli.md\n@@ -140,7 +140,11 @@ as current, but named so a reviewer can tell carried-forward evidence apart.\n   records a finding. Every finding needs a severity and an action. `resolved`\n   and `wont-fix` close it; `accepted-risk` closes it only for a severity the\n   sealed gates accept and only with an owner, a rationale and a review date;\n-  `open` and `changes-requested` block closeout.\n+  `within-limit` closes it at any severity, only with the id of a limit the\n+  spec declares under `### Accepted limits` and the bundle sealed (its\n+  `limit` field) and a rationale; `open` and `changes-requested` block\n+  closeout. A reviewer records it from a follow-up whose `limit` names that\n+  sealed limit.\n - The decision must be `approved`, or `approved-with-reservations` where the\n   sealed gates allow it.\n \ndiff --git a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex f7b6d52d..10e06a2f 100644\n--- a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -1072,7 +1072,7 @@ Acrescente `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` a uma solicit\n \n **Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar; o `review-brief/9` também manda rodar cada comando `pose` do plano como `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, porque o `pose` que o shell do revisor resolve pode ser um motor mais antigo. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\n \n-**Limites aceitos.** Uma spec pode declarar os limites residuais das suas garantias em `### Accepted limits`, um `- L<N>: <suposição>. Accepted because <motivo>.` por limite, que pode ocupar várias linhas (blocos de código e comentários HTML não são lidos como declarações); `lint-spec --ready-check` recusa um limite que não diz por que é aceito e, quando um ADR cita a spec, um que o ADR não nomeia. O plano de review e o bundle selam os limites (`accepted_limits`, parte do digest do plano; num escopo com várias specs cada id é qualificado como `<spec>#L<N>`), e o brief do template `review-brief/11` os lista: um achado que cai dentro de um deles é um follow-up com `limit` igual ao seu id, registrado na atestação com a disposição `within-limit`, que não bloqueia uma aprovação seja qual for a severidade e é contado à parte nos resultados de governança; um limite que o bundle não selou, ou um achado `within-limit` sem motivo, é recusado. Um revisor que ache um limite injustificado relata um achado que o contesta, que bloqueia como qualquer achado. Uma review converge classificando o que foi declarado fora do escopo, não por um teto de rodadas.\n+**Limites aceitos.** Uma spec pode declarar os limites residuais das suas garantias em `### Accepted limits`, um `- L<N>: <suposição>. Accepted because <motivo>.` por limite, cujas linhas de continuação ficam indentadas sob ele; a seção contém apenas limites, e qualquer outra linha nela é reportada e deixa sem selo o limite a que pertence (comentários HTML não são lidos); `lint-spec --ready-check` recusa um limite que não diz por que é aceito e, quando um ADR cita a spec, um que o ADR não nomeia. O plano de review e o bundle selam os limites (`accepted_limits`, parte do digest do plano; num escopo com várias specs cada id é qualificado como `<spec>#L<N>`), e o brief do template `review-brief/11` os lista: um achado que cai dentro de um deles é um follow-up com `limit` igual ao seu id, registrado na atestação com a disposição `within-limit`, que não bloqueia uma aprovação seja qual for a severidade e é contado à parte nos resultados de governança; um limite que o bundle não selou, ou um achado `within-limit` sem motivo, é recusado. Um revisor que ache um limite injustificado relata um achado que o contesta, que bloqueia como qualquer achado. Uma review converge classificando o que foi declarado fora do escopo, não por um teto de rodadas.\n \n **Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** No Linux o envio roda o adaptador sob um shim de run de review, o mesmo executável reentrado, que é o subreaper da subárvore do run: depois que o adaptador termina, num timeout e quando o envio termina, ele encerra e recolhe todo processo que restou nessa subárvore, mesmo um que abriu sessão própria e limpou o ambiente, e relata os pids encerrados por um pipe que o revisor não herda; o envio os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza. Quem embute o motor sem ativar o shim recorre a encerrar os processos cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); nas outras plataformas Unix só o grupo de processos é encerrado, e no Windows o adaptador roda num job object com encerramento ao fechar, que o envio termina depois do run, num timeout e, pelo limite do job, quando o envio termina. O brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. O envio marca a tentativa como ativa antes de publicar a reserva, então um envio que termina antes de registrar o run sempre deixa uma tentativa parada, que `pose delegate ls` lista com a causa, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo; uma liberação roda por vez, sob um lock que o sistema libera junto com quem o detém, e uma liberação conclui com qualquer run que a tentativa já registrou em vez de gravar outro. Uma reserva que um motor anterior deixou sem marcador não registra dono: `pose delegate ls` a lista depois de dez minutos, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa> --confirm-abandoned` a libera com a confirmação do operador de que nenhum envio dela está rodando.\n \ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\nindex 30c3de6d..713f2261 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n@@ -22,7 +22,7 @@ type ReviewAcceptedLimit struct {\n \tSpec string `json:\"spec,omitempty\"`\n }\n \n-var acceptedLimitRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^[-*]\\s+(L[1-9][0-9]*):\\s+(.+)$`)\n+var acceptedLimitRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^[-*+]\\s+(L[1-9][0-9]*):\\s+(.+)$`)\n \n // acceptedLimitMarkerRE finds the marker where a sentence opens, after the\n // assumption: \"<assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.\" A marker inside a\n@@ -59,66 +59,81 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \tvar problems []string\n \tin := false\n \tseen := map[string]bool{}\n-\t// An entry is one Markdown list item: it may wrap over indented lines,\n-\t// including after a blank line, and it ends at an unindented line or the\n-\t// next item\n-\t// (review rounds 8 N1 and 9 N2). Fenced blocks and HTML comments are not\n-\t// the spec's declarations, so examples there are never sealed (round 8\n-\t// N2); a fence closes only with its own marker, at least as long.\n-\ttype entry struct{ id, text string }\n+\t// The section is read fail-closed (review rounds 8 to 10): it holds only\n+\t// limits, each one list item whose wrapped lines are indented deeper than\n+\t// its marker (also after a blank line). Any other line in the section\n+\t// (prose, a lazy unindented continuation, a nested list, a fence, another\n+\t// kind of item) is reported, and the limit it belongs to or follows is\n+\t// not sealed: the engine never seals a limit it may have read in part.\n+\t// Fenced blocks and HTML comments outside the section are not the spec's\n+\t// declarations, so examples there are never read.\n+\ttype entry struct {\n+\t\tid, text string\n+\t\tindent   int\n+\t\tbad      bool\n+\t}\n \tvar entries []entry\n-\topen := false\n+\treport := func(line, why string) {\n+\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limits: \"+quoteLedgerText(strings.TrimSpace(line))+\" \"+why)\n+\t\tif len(entries) > 0 && !entries[len(entries)-1].bad {\n+\t\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].bad = true\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\ttext := body\n+\tif start := strings.Index(text, \"<!--\"); start >= 0 && !strings.Contains(text[start:], \"-->\") {\n+\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limits: an unterminated HTML comment hides the rest of the spec\")\n+\t}\n+\ttext = stripHTMLComments(text)\n \tfence := \"\"\n-\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(stripHTMLComments(body), \"\\n\") {\n+\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(text, \"\\n\") {\n \t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line)\n+\t\tindent := len(line) - len(strings.TrimLeft(line, \" \\t\"))\n \t\tif marker := fenceMarker(trimmed); marker != \"\" {\n+\t\t\tif in {\n+\t\t\t\treport(line, \"is a code fence; examples belong outside the section\")\n+\t\t\t}\n \t\t\tswitch {\n \t\t\tcase fence == \"\":\n \t\t\t\tfence = marker\n \t\t\tcase marker[0] == fence[0] && len(marker) >= len(fence) && strings.TrimLeft(trimmed, marker[:1]) == \"\":\n \t\t\t\tfence = \"\"\n \t\t\t}\n-\t\t\topen = false\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n \t\tif fence != \"\" {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tindented := trimmed != \"\" && (strings.HasPrefix(line, \" \") || strings.HasPrefix(line, \"\\t\"))\n-\t\tif !indented && strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"#\") {\n-\t\t\theading := strings.TrimSpace(strings.TrimLeft(trimmed, \"#\"))\n+\t\tif indent <= 3 && markdownHeadingRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n+\t\t\theading := strings.TrimSpace(strings.Trim(trimmed, \"# \"))\n \t\t\tin = strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Accepted limits\") || strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Limites aceitos\")\n-\t\t\topen = false\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tif !in {\n+\t\tif !in || trimmed == \"\" {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tswitch {\n-\t\tcase trimmed == \"\":\n-\t\t\t// A blank line inside an item keeps it open: an indented\n-\t\t\t// paragraph after it still belongs to the item, and an\n-\t\t\t// unindented line ends it.\n-\t\tcase !indented && (strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"- \") || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"* \")):\n-\t\t\tm := acceptedLimitRE.FindStringSubmatch(trimmed)\n-\t\t\tif m == nil {\n-\t\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+quoteLedgerText(trimmed)+\" is not written as '- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.'\")\n-\t\t\t\topen = false\n+\t\t// A line indented under the current limit belongs to it: a list\n+\t\t// there is nested, anything else continues the limit.\n+\t\tunder := len(entries) > 0 && indent > entries[len(entries)-1].indent\n+\t\tif under && listItemRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n+\t\t\treport(line, \"is a nested list; write the limit as one item\")\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif !under && indent <= 3 {\n+\t\t\tif m := acceptedLimitRE.FindStringSubmatch(trimmed); m != nil {\n+\t\t\t\tentries = append(entries, entry{id: m[1], text: strings.TrimSpace(m[2]), indent: indent})\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tif listItemRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n+\t\t\t\treport(line, \"is not written as '- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.'\")\n+\t\t\t\tentries = append(entries, entry{bad: true})\n \t\t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t\t}\n-\t\t\tentries = append(entries, entry{id: m[1], text: strings.TrimSpace(m[2])})\n-\t\t\topen = true\n-\t\tcase indented && open && (strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"- \") || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"* \") || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"+ \")):\n-\t\t\t// A nested list cannot be read as one sentence: the whole limit\n-\t\t\t// is refused and not sealed.\n-\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+entries[len(entries)-1].id+\" holds a nested list; write it as one item\")\n-\t\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].id = \"\"\n-\t\t\topen = false\n-\t\tcase indented && open:\n-\t\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].text += \" \" + trimmed\n-\t\tdefault:\n-\t\t\topen = false\n \t\t}\n+\t\tif !under {\n+\t\t\treport(line, \"is neither a limit nor indented under one; the section holds only limits\")\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].text += \" \" + trimmed\n \t}\n \tfor _, e := range entries {\n \t\tif e.id == \"\" {\n@@ -129,6 +144,10 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n \t\tseen[e.id] = true\n+\t\tif e.bad {\n+\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+e.id+\" is not sealed: part of it could not be read\")\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n \t\tif !hasAcceptedLimitReason(e.text) {\n \t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+e.id+\" does not say why it is accepted ('Accepted because ...')\")\n \t\t\tcontinue\n@@ -138,14 +157,24 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \treturn limits, problems\n }\n \n-// fenceMarker returns the run of backticks or tildes that opens a code\n-// fence on trimmed, or \"\".\n+var (\n+\tmarkdownHeadingRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^#{1,6}(\\s|$)`)\n+\tlistItemRE        = regexp.MustCompile(`^([-*+]|[0-9]+[.)])(\\s|$)`)\n+)\n+\n+// fenceMarker returns the run of backticks or tildes that opens or closes a\n+// code fence on trimmed, or \"\". A backtick run followed by more backticks on\n+// the line is inline code, not a fence.\n func fenceMarker(trimmed string) string {\n \tfor _, c := range []string{\"`\", \"~\"} {\n \t\tn := len(trimmed) - len(strings.TrimLeft(trimmed, c))\n-\t\tif n >= 3 {\n-\t\t\treturn trimmed[:n]\n+\t\tif n < 3 {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif c == \"`\" && strings.Contains(trimmed[n:], \"`\") {\n+\t\t\treturn \"\"\n \t\t}\n+\t\treturn trimmed[:n]\n \t}\n \treturn \"\"\n }\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go\nindex e3992928..61ac4564 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go\n@@ -153,7 +153,7 @@ func TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits(t *testing.T) {\n // Review round 8, N1, N2: a wrapped entry keeps its continuation; fenced and\n // commented examples are not sealed.\n func TestAcceptedLimitsReadWrappedEntriesAndSkipExamples(t *testing.T) {\n-\tbody := \"### Accepted limits\\n- L1: An escape. Accepted because a shim covers it\\n  only on Linux and only for children.\\n\\n```\\n- L2: An example. Accepted because it is only shown.\\n```\\n<!--\\n- L3: A hidden one. Accepted because nobody sees it.\\n-->\\n\"\n+\tbody := \"### Accepted limits\\n- L1: An escape. Accepted because a shim covers it\\n  only on Linux and only for children.\\n<!--\\n- L3: A hidden one. Accepted because nobody sees it.\\n-->\\n### Notes\\n```\\n### Accepted limits\\n- L2: An example. Accepted because it is only shown.\\n```\\n\"\n \tlimits, problems := ParseAcceptedLimits(body)\n \tif len(problems) != 0 || len(limits) != 1 {\n \t\tt.Fatalf(\"limits %+v problems %v\", limits, problems)\n@@ -173,12 +173,22 @@ func TestAcceptedLimitsEndWhereTheirItemEnds(t *testing.T) {\n \t\tproblem    string\n \t}{\n \t\t{\"wrapped hash\", \"### Accepted limits\\n- L1: An escape is open. Accepted because see issue\\n  #42 only on Linux.\\n\", map[string]string{\"L1\": \"An escape is open. Accepted because see issue #42 only on Linux.\"}, \"\"},\n-\t\t{\"nested list\", \"### Accepted limits\\n- L1: An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare\\n  - only on Linux\\n\", map[string]string{}, \"nested list\"},\n-\t\t{\"later paragraph\", \"### Accepted limits\\n- L1: An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare.\\n\\nSome note that follows the list.\\n* L2: Another escape. Accepted because it is rarer.\\n\", map[string]string{\"L1\": \"An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare.\", \"L2\": \"Another escape. Accepted because it is rarer.\"}, \"\"},\n \t\t{\"indented paragraph\", \"### Accepted limits\\n- L1: An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare\\n\\n  and only on Linux.\\n\", map[string]string{\"L1\": \"An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare and only on Linux.\"}, \"\"},\n-\t\t{\"borrowed reason\", \"### Accepted limits\\n- L1: no reason here\\n\\nSome unrelated note. Accepted because other words follow.\\n\", map[string]string{}, \"does not say why\"},\n-\t\t{\"four backticks\", \"### Accepted limits\\n````\\n```\\n- L1: An example escape. Accepted because it is only shown.\\n```\\n````\\n\", map[string]string{}, \"\"},\n-\t\t{\"tilde fence\", \"### Accepted limits\\n~~~\\n```\\n- L1: An example escape. Accepted because it is only shown.\\n~~~\\n\", map[string]string{}, \"\"},\n+\t\t{\"nested list\", \"### Accepted limits\\n- L1: An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare\\n  - only on Linux\\n\", map[string]string{}, \"nested list\"},\n+\t\t{\"later paragraph\", \"### Accepted limits\\n- L1: An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare.\\n\\nSome note that follows the list.\\n* L2: Another escape. Accepted because it is rarer.\\n\", map[string]string{\"L2\": \"Another escape. Accepted because it is rarer.\"}, \"holds only limits\"},\n+\t\t{\"borrowed reason\", \"### Accepted limits\\n- L1: no reason here\\n\\nSome unrelated note. Accepted because other words follow.\\n\", map[string]string{}, \"holds only limits\"},\n+\t\t// Review round 10, N1: a lazy continuation and a fence inside an\n+\t\t// item are reported, and the limit is not sealed in part.\n+\t\t{\"lazy continuation\", \"### Accepted limits\\n- L1: An escape is open. Accepted because a shim covers it\\nonly on Linux and only for children.\\n\", map[string]string{}, \"is not sealed\"},\n+\t\t{\"fence in item\", \"### Accepted limits\\n- L1: An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare\\n  ```\\n  code\\n  ```\\n  and only on Linux.\\n\", map[string]string{}, \"code fence\"},\n+\t\t{\"four backticks\", \"### Accepted limits\\n````\\n```\\n- L1: An example escape. Accepted because it is only shown.\\n```\\n````\\n\", map[string]string{}, \"code fence\"},\n+\t\t// Review round 10, N2: valid list forms are read; others are reported.\n+\t\t{\"indented items\", \"  ### Accepted limits\\n  - L1: An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare.\\n+ L2: Another escape. Accepted because it is rarer.\\n\", map[string]string{\"L1\": \"An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare.\", \"L2\": \"Another escape. Accepted because it is rarer.\"}, \"\"},\n+\t\t{\"ordered item\", \"### Accepted limits\\n1. L1: An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare.\\n\", map[string]string{}, \"is not written as\"},\n+\t\t// Review round 10, N4: inline code is no fence; an unterminated\n+\t\t// comment is reported.\n+\t\t{\"inline backticks\", \"### Notes\\n```a``` is inline\\n### Accepted limits\\n- L1: An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare.\\n\", map[string]string{\"L1\": \"An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare.\"}, \"\"},\n+\t\t{\"unterminated comment\", \"<!-- draft\\n### Accepted limits\\n- L1: An escape is open. Accepted because it is rare.\\n\", map[string]string{}, \"unterminated HTML comment\"},\n \t}\n \tfor _, c := range cases {\n \t\tlimits, problems := ParseAcceptedLimits(c.body)\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\nindex 759c578d..b71a4951 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n+++","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/home/go/.claude/projects/-var-tmp-pose-work-go-pose-review-run-349050671-worktree/[private-session]/tool-results/b57bfsdat.txt","persistedOutputSize":42064},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DuruqawybsnscTrPHCXP2B","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQzrw4YKttLBsxz1USft","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0186QbDjeRGLj2ZmiTCk611d","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"git diff 6e79f8af6b34d17d159777d337273d32131c7f8f 93715d8c229fee6c0cb004d46d6efc4a0550efd2 -- POSE.md docs-site locales pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go | cat"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5502,"cache_read_input_tokens":43918,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5502},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"cb4d3b23-3f0a-445b-bdcc-fd6fdff1aa3e","timestamp":"2026-11-26T05:36:47.981Z","request_id":"req_011ChQzrvsPBjRjepzQGEDup","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0186QbDjeRGLj2ZmiTCk611d":{"command":"git diff 6e79f8af6b34d17d159777d337273d32131c7f8f 93715d8c229fee6c0cb004d46d6efc4a0550efd2 -- POSE.md docs-site locales pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go | cat"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0186QbDjeRGLj2ZmiTCk611d","type":"tool_result","content":"diff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 57f95eef..a8bb2a8d 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -1075,7 +1075,7 @@ Attach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `po\n \n **Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n \n-**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit, which may wrap over several lines (fenced blocks and HTML comments are not read as declarations); `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity and is counted apart in the governance outcomes; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\n+**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit, whose wrapped lines are indented under it; the section holds only limits, and any other line in it is reported and leaves the limit it belongs to unsealed (HTML comments are not read); `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity and is counted apart in the governance outcomes; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\n \n **Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux the dispatch runs the adapter under a review run shim, the same executable re-entered, which is the subreaper of the run's subtree: after the adapter exits, on a timeout, and when its dispatcher ends, it ends and reaps every process left in that subtree, even one that started its own session and cleared its environment, and reports the ended pids through a pipe the reviewer does not inherit; the dispatch lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory. An embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to ending the processes whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); on other Unix platforms only the process group is ended, and on Windows the adapter runs in a kill-on-close job object that the dispatch terminates after the run, on a timeout and, through the job's limit, when the dispatcher ends. The brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, under a lock the system releases with its holder, and a release completes with any run the attempt already recorded instead of writing another. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\n \ndiff --git a/docs-site/docs/cli.md b/docs-site/docs/cli.md\nindex 1611698b..212c45f8 100644\n--- a/docs-site/docs/cli.md\n+++ b/docs-site/docs/cli.md\n@@ -140,7 +140,11 @@ as current, but named so a reviewer can tell carried-forward evidence apart.\n   records a finding. Every finding needs a severity and an action. `resolved`\n   and `wont-fix` close it; `accepted-risk` closes it only for a severity the\n   sealed gates accept and only with an owner, a rationale and a review date;\n-  `open` and `changes-requested` block closeout.\n+  `within-limit` closes it at any severity, only with the id of a limit the\n+  spec declares under `### Accepted limits` and the bundle sealed (its\n+  `limit` field) and a rationale; `open` and `changes-requested` block\n+  closeout. A reviewer records it from a follow-up whose `limit` names that\n+  sealed limit.\n - The decision must be `approved`, or `approved-with-reservations` where the\n   sealed gates allow it.\n \ndiff --git a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex f7b6d52d..10e06a2f 100644\n--- a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -1072,7 +1072,7 @@ Acrescente `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` a uma solicit\n \n **Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar; o `review-brief/9` também manda rodar cada comando `pose` do plano como `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, porque o `pose` que o shell do revisor resolve pode ser um motor mais antigo. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\n \n-**Limites aceitos.** Uma spec pode declarar os limites residuais das suas garantias em `### Accepted limits`, um `- L<N>: <suposição>. Accepted because <motivo>.` por limite, que pode ocupar várias linhas (blocos de código e comentários HTML não são lidos como declarações); `lint-spec --ready-check` recusa um limite que não diz por que é aceito e, quando um ADR cita a spec, um que o ADR não nomeia. O plano de review e o bundle selam os limites (`accepted_limits`, parte do digest do plano; num escopo com várias specs cada id é qualificado como `<spec>#L<N>`), e o brief do template `review-brief/11` os lista: um achado que cai dentro de um deles é um follow-up com `limit` igual ao seu id, registrado na atestação com a disposição `within-limit`, que não bloqueia uma aprovação seja qual for a severidade e é contado à parte nos resultados de governança; um limite que o bundle não selou, ou um achado `within-limit` sem motivo, é recusado. Um revisor que ache um limite injustificado relata um achado que o contesta, que bloqueia como qualquer achado. Uma review converge classificando o que foi declarado fora do escopo, não por um teto de rodadas.\n+**Limites aceitos.** Uma spec pode declarar os limites residuais das suas garantias em `### Accepted limits`, um `- L<N>: <suposição>. Accepted because <motivo>.` por limite, cujas linhas de continuação ficam indentadas sob ele; a seção contém apenas limites, e qualquer outra linha nela é reportada e deixa sem selo o limite a que pertence (comentários HTML não são lidos); `lint-spec --ready-check` recusa um limite que não diz por que é aceito e, quando um ADR cita a spec, um que o ADR não nomeia. O plano de review e o bundle selam os limites (`accepted_limits`, parte do digest do plano; num escopo com várias specs cada id é qualificado como `<spec>#L<N>`), e o brief do template `review-brief/11` os lista: um achado que cai dentro de um deles é um follow-up com `limit` igual ao seu id, registrado na atestação com a disposição `within-limit`, que não bloqueia uma aprovação seja qual for a severidade e é contado à parte nos resultados de governança; um limite que o bundle não selou, ou um achado `within-limit` sem motivo, é recusado. Um revisor que ache um limite injustificado relata um achado que o contesta, que bloqueia como qualquer achado. Uma review converge classificando o que foi declarado fora do escopo, não por um teto de rodadas.\n \n **Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** No Linux o envio roda o adaptador sob um shim de run de review, o mesmo executável reentrado, que é o subreaper da subárvore do run: depois que o adaptador termina, num timeout e quando o envio termina, ele encerra e recolhe todo processo que restou nessa subárvore, mesmo um que abriu sessão própria e limpou o ambiente, e relata os pids encerrados por um pipe que o revisor não herda; o envio os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza. Quem embute o motor sem ativar o shim recorre a encerrar os processos cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); nas outras plataformas Unix só o grupo de processos é encerrado, e no Windows o adaptador roda num job object com encerramento ao fechar, que o envio termina depois do run, num timeout e, pelo limite do job, quando o envio termina. O brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. O envio marca a tentativa como ativa antes de publicar a reserva, então um envio que termina antes de registrar o run sempre deixa uma tentativa parada, que `pose delegate ls` lista com a causa, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo; uma liberação roda por vez, sob um lock que o sistema libera junto com quem o detém, e uma liberação conclui com qualquer run que a tentativa já registrou em vez de gravar outro. Uma reserva que um motor anterior deixou sem marcador não registra dono: `pose delegate ls` a lista depois de dez minutos, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa> --confirm-abandoned` a libera com a confirmação do operador de que nenhum envio dela está rodando.\n \ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\nindex 30c3de6d..713f2261 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n@@ -22,7 +22,7 @@ type ReviewAcceptedLimit struct {\n \tSpec string `json:\"spec,omitempty\"`\n }\n \n-var acceptedLimitRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^[-*]\\s+(L[1-9][0-9]*):\\s+(.+)$`)\n+var acceptedLimitRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^[-*+]\\s+(L[1-9][0-9]*):\\s+(.+)$`)\n \n // acceptedLimitMarkerRE finds the marker where a sentence opens, after the\n // assumption: \"<assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.\" A marker inside a\n@@ -59,66 +59,81 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \tvar problems []string\n \tin := false\n \tseen := map[string]bool{}\n-\t// An entry is one Markdown list item: it may wrap over indented lines,\n-\t// including after a blank line, and it ends at an unindented line or the\n-\t// next item\n-\t// (review rounds 8 N1 and 9 N2). Fenced blocks and HTML comments are not\n-\t// the spec's declarations, so examples there are never sealed (round 8\n-\t// N2); a fence closes only with its own marker, at least as long.\n-\ttype entry struct{ id, text string }\n+\t// The section is read fail-closed (review rounds 8 to 10): it holds only\n+\t// limits, each one list item whose wrapped lines are indented deeper than\n+\t// its marker (also after a blank line). Any other line in the section\n+\t// (prose, a lazy unindented continuation, a nested list, a fence, another\n+\t// kind of item) is reported, and the limit it belongs to or follows is\n+\t// not sealed: the engine never seals a limit it may have read in part.\n+\t// Fenced blocks and HTML comments outside the section are not the spec's\n+\t// declarations, so examples there are never read.\n+\ttype entry struct {\n+\t\tid, text string\n+\t\tindent   int\n+\t\tbad      bool\n+\t}\n \tvar entries []entry\n-\topen := false\n+\treport := func(line, why string) {\n+\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limits: \"+quoteLedgerText(strings.TrimSpace(line))+\" \"+why)\n+\t\tif len(entries) > 0 && !entries[len(entries)-1].bad {\n+\t\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].bad = true\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\ttext := body\n+\tif start := strings.Index(text, \"<!--\"); start >= 0 && !strings.Contains(text[start:], \"-->\") {\n+\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limits: an unterminated HTML comment hides the rest of the spec\")\n+\t}\n+\ttext = stripHTMLComments(text)\n \tfence := \"\"\n-\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(stripHTMLComments(body), \"\\n\") {\n+\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(text, \"\\n\") {\n \t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line)\n+\t\tindent := len(line) - len(strings.TrimLeft(line, \" \\t\"))\n \t\tif marker := fenceMarker(trimmed); marker != \"\" {\n+\t\t\tif in {\n+\t\t\t\treport(line, \"is a code fence; examples belong outside the section\")\n+\t\t\t}\n \t\t\tswitch {\n \t\t\tcase fence == \"\":\n \t\t\t\tfence = marker\n \t\t\tcase marker[0] == fence[0] && len(marker) >= len(fence) && strings.TrimLeft(trimmed, marker[:1]) == \"\":\n \t\t\t\tfence = \"\"\n \t\t\t}\n-\t\t\topen = false\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n \t\tif fence != \"\" {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tindented := trimmed != \"\" && (strings.HasPrefix(line, \" \") || strings.HasPrefix(line, \"\\t\"))\n-\t\tif !indented && strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"#\") {\n-\t\t\theading := strings.TrimSpace(strings.TrimLeft(trimmed, \"#\"))\n+\t\tif indent <= 3 && markdownHeadingRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n+\t\t\theading := strings.TrimSpace(strings.Trim(trimmed, \"# \"))\n \t\t\tin = strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Accepted limits\") || strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Limites aceitos\")\n-\t\t\topen = false\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tif !in {\n+\t\tif !in || trimmed == \"\" {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tswitch {\n-\t\tcase trimmed == \"\":\n-\t\t\t// A blank line inside an item keeps it open: an indented\n-\t\t\t// paragraph after it still belongs to the item, and an\n-\t\t\t// unindented line ends it.\n-\t\tcase !indented && (strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"- \") || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"* \")):\n-\t\t\tm := acceptedLimitRE.FindStringSubmatch(trimmed)\n-\t\t\tif m == nil {\n-\t\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+quoteLedgerText(trimmed)+\" is not written as '- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.'\")\n-\t\t\t\topen = false\n+\t\t// A line indented under the current limit belongs to it: a list\n+\t\t// there is nested, anything else continues the limit.\n+\t\tunder := len(entries) > 0 && indent > entries[len(entries)-1].indent\n+\t\tif under && listItemRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n+\t\t\treport(line, \"is a nested list; write the limit as one item\")\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif !under && indent <= 3 {\n+\t\t\tif m := acceptedLimitRE.FindStringSubmatch(trimmed); m != nil {\n+\t\t\t\tentries = append(entries, entry{id: m[1], text: strings.TrimSpace(m[2]), indent: indent})\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tif listItemRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n+\t\t\t\treport(line, \"is not written as '- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.'\")\n+\t\t\t\tentries = append(entries, entry{bad: true})\n \t\t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t\t}\n-\t\t\tentries = append(entries, entry{id: m[1], text: strings.TrimSpace(m[2])})\n-\t\t\topen = true\n-\t\tcase indented && open && (strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"- \") || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"* \") || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"+ \")):\n-\t\t\t// A nested list cannot be read as one sentence: the whole limit\n-\t\t\t// is refused and not sealed.\n-\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+entries[len(entries)-1].id+\" holds a nested list; write it as one item\")\n-\t\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].id = \"\"\n-\t\t\topen = false\n-\t\tcase indented && open:\n-\t\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].text += \" \" + trimmed\n-\t\tdefault:\n-\t\t\topen = false\n \t\t}\n+\t\tif !under {\n+\t\t\treport(line, \"is neither a limit nor indented under one; the section holds only limits\")\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].text += \" \" + trimmed\n \t}\n \tfor _, e := range entries {\n \t\tif e.id == \"\" {\n@@ -129,6 +144,10 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n \t\tseen[e.id] = true\n+\t\tif e.bad {\n+\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+e.id+\" is not sealed: part of it could not be read\")\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n \t\tif !hasAcceptedLimitReason(e.text) {\n \t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+e.id+\" does not say why it is accepted ('Accepted because ...')\")\n \t\t\tcontinue\n@@ -138,14 +157,24 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \treturn limits, problems\n }\n \n-// fenceMarker returns the run of backticks or tildes that opens a code\n-// fence on trimmed, or \"\".\n+var (\n+\tmarkdownHeadingRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^#{1,6}(\\s|$)`)\n+\tlistItemRE        = regexp.MustCompile(`^([-*+]|[0-9]+[.)])(\\s|$)`)\n+)\n+\n+// fenceMarker returns the run of backticks or tildes that opens or closes a\n+// code fence on trimmed, or \"\". A backtick run followed by more backticks on\n+// the line is inline code, not a fence.\n func fenceMarker(trimmed string) string {\n \tfor _, c := range []string{\"`\", \"~\"} {\n \t\tn := len(trimmed) - len(strings.TrimLeft(trimmed, c))\n-\t\tif n >= 3 {\n-\t\t\treturn trimmed[:n]\n+\t\tif n < 3 {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif c == \"`\" && strings.Contains(trimmed[n:], \"`\") {\n+\t\t\treturn \"\"\n \t\t}\n+\t\treturn trimmed[:n]\n \t}\n \treturn \"\"\n }","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"db67390b-8c38-458d-b403-1af128c4d7d3","timestamp":"2026-11-26T05:36:48.038Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"diff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 57f95eef..a8bb2a8d 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -1075,7 +1075,7 @@ Attach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `po\n \n **Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n \n-**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit, which may wrap over several lines (fenced blocks and HTML comments are not read as declarations); `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity and is counted apart in the governance outcomes; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\n+**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit, whose wrapped lines are indented under it; the section holds only limits, and any other line in it is reported and leaves the limit it belongs to unsealed (HTML comments are not read); `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity and is counted apart in the governance outcomes; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\n \n **Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux the dispatch runs the adapter under a review run shim, the same executable re-entered, which is the subreaper of the run's subtree: after the adapter exits, on a timeout, and when its dispatcher ends, it ends and reaps every process left in that subtree, even one that started its own session and cleared its environment, and reports the ended pids through a pipe the reviewer does not inherit; the dispatch lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory. An embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to ending the processes whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); on other Unix platforms only the process group is ended, and on Windows the adapter runs in a kill-on-close job object that the dispatch terminates after the run, on a timeout and, through the job's limit, when the dispatcher ends. The brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, under a lock the system releases with its holder, and a release completes with any run the attempt already recorded instead of writing another. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\n \ndiff --git a/docs-site/docs/cli.md b/docs-site/docs/cli.md\nindex 1611698b..212c45f8 100644\n--- a/docs-site/docs/cli.md\n+++ b/docs-site/docs/cli.md\n@@ -140,7 +140,11 @@ as current, but named so a reviewer can tell carried-forward evidence apart.\n   records a finding. Every finding needs a severity and an action. `resolved`\n   and `wont-fix` close it; `accepted-risk` closes it only for a severity the\n   sealed gates accept and only with an owner, a rationale and a review date;\n-  `open` and `changes-requested` block closeout.\n+  `within-limit` closes it at any severity, only with the id of a limit the\n+  spec declares under `### Accepted limits` and the bundle sealed (its\n+  `limit` field) and a rationale; `open` and `changes-requested` block\n+  closeout. A reviewer records it from a follow-up whose `limit` names that\n+  sealed limit.\n - The decision must be `approved`, or `approved-with-reservations` where the\n   sealed gates allow it.\n \ndiff --git a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex f7b6d52d..10e06a2f 100644\n--- a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -1072,7 +1072,7 @@ Acrescente `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` a uma solicit\n \n **Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar; o `review-brief/9` também manda rodar cada comando `pose` do plano como `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, porque o `pose` que o shell do revisor resolve pode ser um motor mais antigo. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\n \n-**Limites aceitos.** Uma spec pode declarar os limites residuais das suas garantias em `### Accepted limits`, um `- L<N>: <suposição>. Accepted because <motivo>.` por limite, que pode ocupar várias linhas (blocos de código e comentários HTML não são lidos como declarações); `lint-spec --ready-check` recusa um limite que não diz por que é aceito e, quando um ADR cita a spec, um que o ADR não nomeia. O plano de review e o bundle selam os limites (`accepted_limits`, parte do digest do plano; num escopo com várias specs cada id é qualificado como `<spec>#L<N>`), e o brief do template `review-brief/11` os lista: um achado que cai dentro de um deles é um follow-up com `limit` igual ao seu id, registrado na atestação com a disposição `within-limit`, que não bloqueia uma aprovação seja qual for a severidade e é contado à parte nos resultados de governança; um limite que o bundle não selou, ou um achado `within-limit` sem motivo, é recusado. Um revisor que ache um limite injustificado relata um achado que o contesta, que bloqueia como qualquer achado. Uma review converge classificando o que foi declarado fora do escopo, não por um teto de rodadas.\n+**Limites aceitos.** Uma spec pode declarar os limites residuais das suas garantias em `### Accepted limits`, um `- L<N>: <suposição>. Accepted because <motivo>.` por limite, cujas linhas de continuação ficam indentadas sob ele; a seção contém apenas limites, e qualquer outra linha nela é reportada e deixa sem selo o limite a que pertence (comentários HTML não são lidos); `lint-spec --ready-check` recusa um limite que não diz por que é aceito e, quando um ADR cita a spec, um que o ADR não nomeia. O plano de review e o bundle selam os limites (`accepted_limits`, parte do digest do plano; num escopo com várias specs cada id é qualificado como `<spec>#L<N>`), e o brief do template `review-brief/11` os lista: um achado que cai dentro de um deles é um follow-up com `limit` igual ao seu id, registrado na atestação com a disposição `within-limit`, que não bloqueia uma aprovação seja qual for a severidade e é contado à parte nos resultados de governança; um limite que o bundle não selou, ou um achado `within-limit` sem motivo, é recusado. Um revisor que ache um limite injustificado relata um achado que o contesta, que bloqueia como qualquer achado. Uma review converge classificando o que foi declarado fora do escopo, não por um teto de rodadas.\n \n **Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** No Linux o envio roda o adaptador sob um shim de run de review, o mesmo executável reentrado, que é o subreaper da subárvore do run: depois que o adaptador termina, num timeout e quando o envio termina, ele encerra e recolhe todo processo que restou nessa subárvore, mesmo um que abriu sessão própria e limpou o ambiente, e relata os pids encerrados por um pipe que o revisor não herda; o envio os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza. Quem embute o motor sem ativar o shim recorre a encerrar os processos cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); nas outras plataformas Unix só o grupo de processos é encerrado, e no Windows o adaptador roda num job object com encerramento ao fechar, que o envio termina depois do run, num timeout e, pelo limite do job, quando o envio termina. O brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. O envio marca a tentativa como ativa antes de publicar a reserva, então um envio que termina antes de registrar o run sempre deixa uma tentativa parada, que `pose delegate ls` lista com a causa, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo; uma liberação roda por vez, sob um lock que o sistema libera junto com quem o detém, e uma liberação conclui com qualquer run que a tentativa já registrou em vez de gravar outro. Uma reserva que um motor anterior deixou sem marcador não registra dono: `pose delegate ls` a lista depois de dez minutos, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa> --confirm-abandoned` a libera com a confirmação do operador de que nenhum envio dela está rodando.\n \ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\nindex 30c3de6d..713f2261 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n@@ -22,7 +22,7 @@ type ReviewAcceptedLimit struct {\n \tSpec string `json:\"spec,omitempty\"`\n }\n \n-var acceptedLimitRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^[-*]\\s+(L[1-9][0-9]*):\\s+(.+)$`)\n+var acceptedLimitRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^[-*+]\\s+(L[1-9][0-9]*):\\s+(.+)$`)\n \n // acceptedLimitMarkerRE finds the marker where a sentence opens, after the\n // assumption: \"<assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.\" A marker inside a\n@@ -59,66 +59,81 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \tvar problems []string\n \tin := false\n \tseen := map[string]bool{}\n-\t// An entry is one Markdown list item: it may wrap over indented lines,\n-\t// including after a blank line, and it ends at an unindented line or the\n-\t// next item\n-\t// (review rounds 8 N1 and 9 N2). Fenced blocks and HTML comments are not\n-\t// the spec's declarations, so examples there are never sealed (round 8\n-\t// N2); a fence closes only with its own marker, at least as long.\n-\ttype entry struct{ id, text string }\n+\t// The section is read fail-closed (review rounds 8 to 10): it holds only\n+\t// limits, each one list item whose wrapped lines are indented deeper than\n+\t// its marker (also after a blank line). Any other line in the section\n+\t// (prose, a lazy unindented continuation, a nested list, a fence, another\n+\t// kind of item) is reported, and the limit it belongs to or follows is\n+\t// not sealed: the engine never seals a limit it may have read in part.\n+\t// Fenced blocks and HTML comments outside the section are not the spec's\n+\t// declarations, so examples there are never read.\n+\ttype entry struct {\n+\t\tid, text string\n+\t\tindent   int\n+\t\tbad      bool\n+\t}\n \tvar entries []entry\n-\topen := false\n+\treport := func(line, why string) {\n+\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limits: \"+quoteLedgerText(strings.TrimSpace(line))+\" \"+why)\n+\t\tif len(entries) > 0 && !entries[len(entries)-1].bad {\n+\t\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].bad = true\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\ttext := body\n+\tif start := strings.Index(text, \"<!--\"); start >= 0 && !strings.Contains(text[start:], \"-->\") {\n+\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limits: an unterminated HTML comment hides the rest of the spec\")\n+\t}\n+\ttext = stripHTMLComments(text)\n \tfence := \"\"\n-\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(stripHTMLComments(body), \"\\n\") {\n+\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(text, \"\\n\") {\n \t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line)\n+\t\tindent := len(line) - len(strings.TrimLeft(line, \" \\t\"))\n \t\tif marker := fenceMarker(trimmed); marker != \"\" {\n+\t\t\tif in {\n+\t\t\t\treport(line, \"is a code fence; examples belong outside the section\")\n+\t\t\t}\n \t\t\tswitch {\n \t\t\tcase fence == \"\":\n \t\t\t\tfence = marker\n \t\t\tcase marker[0] == fence[0] && len(marker) >= len(fence) && strings.TrimLeft(trimmed, marker[:1]) == \"\":\n \t\t\t\tfence = \"\"\n \t\t\t}\n-\t\t\topen = false\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n \t\tif fence != \"\" {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tindented := trimmed != \"\" && (strings.HasPrefix(line, \" \") || strings.HasPrefix(line, \"\\t\"))\n-\t\tif !indented && strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"#\") {\n-\t\t\theading := strings.TrimSpace(strings.TrimLeft(trimmed, \"#\"))\n+\t\tif indent <= 3 && markdownHeadingRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n+\t\t\theading := strings.TrimSpace(strings.Trim(trimmed, \"# \"))\n \t\t\tin = strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Accepted limits\") || strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Limites aceitos\")\n-\t\t\topen = false\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tif !in {\n+\t\tif !in || trimmed == \"\" {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tswitch {\n-\t\tcase trimmed == \"\":\n-\t\t\t// A blank line inside an item keeps it open: an indented\n-\t\t\t// paragraph after it still belongs to the item, and an\n-\t\t\t// unindented line ends it.\n-\t\tcase !indented && (strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"- \") || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"* \")):\n-\t\t\tm := acceptedLimitRE.FindStringSubmatch(trimmed)\n-\t\t\tif m == nil {\n-\t\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+quoteLedgerText(trimmed)+\" is not written as '- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.'\")\n-\t\t\t\topen = false\n+\t\t// A line indented under the current limit belongs to it: a list\n+\t\t// there is nested, anything else continues the limit.\n+\t\tunder := len(entries) > 0 && indent > entries[len(entries)-1].indent\n+\t\tif under && listItemRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n+\t\t\treport(line, \"is a nested list; write the limit as one item\")\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif !under && indent <= 3 {\n+\t\t\tif m := acceptedLimitRE.FindStringSubmatch(trimmed); m != nil {\n+\t\t\t\tentries = append(entries, entry{id: m[1], text: strings.TrimSpace(m[2]), indent: indent})\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tif listItemRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n+\t\t\t\treport(line, \"is not written as '- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.'\")\n+\t\t\t\tentries = append(entries, entry{bad: true})\n \t\t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t\t}\n-\t\t\tentries = append(entries, entry{id: m[1], text: strings.TrimSpace(m[2])})\n-\t\t\topen = true\n-\t\tcase indented && open && (strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"- \") || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"* \") || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"+ \")):\n-\t\t\t// A nested list cannot be read as one sentence: the whole limit\n-\t\t\t// is refused and not sealed.\n-\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+entries[len(entries)-1].id+\" holds a nested list; write it as one item\")\n-\t\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].id = \"\"\n-\t\t\topen = false\n-\t\tcase indented && open:\n-\t\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].text += \" \" + trimmed\n-\t\tdefault:\n-\t\t\topen = false\n \t\t}\n+\t\tif !under {\n+\t\t\treport(line, \"is neither a limit nor indented under one; the section holds only limits\")\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].text += \" \" + trimmed\n \t}\n \tfor _, e := range entries {\n \t\tif e.id == \"\" {\n@@ -129,6 +144,10 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n \t\tseen[e.id] = true\n+\t\tif e.bad {\n+\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+e.id+\" is not sealed: part of it could not be read\")\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n \t\tif !hasAcceptedLimitReason(e.text) {\n \t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+e.id+\" does not say why it is accepted ('Accepted because ...')\")\n \t\t\tcontinue\n@@ -138,14 +157,24 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \treturn limits, problems\n }\n \n-// fenceMarker returns the run of backticks or tildes that opens a code\n-// fence on trimmed, or \"\".\n+var (\n+\tmarkdownHeadingRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^#{1,6}(\\s|$)`)\n+\tlistItemRE        = regexp.MustCompile(`^([-*+]|[0-9]+[.)])(\\s|$)`)\n+)\n+\n+// fenceMarker returns the run of backticks or tildes that opens or closes a\n+// code fence on trimmed, or \"\". A backtick run followed by more backticks on\n+// the line is inline code, not a fence.\n func fenceMarker(trimmed string) string {\n \tfor _, c := range []string{\"`\", \"~\"} {\n \t\tn := len(trimmed) - len(strings.TrimLeft(trimmed, c))\n-\t\tif n >= 3 {\n-\t\t\treturn trimmed[:n]\n+\t\tif n < 3 {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif c == \"`\" && strings.Contains(trimmed[n:], \"`\") {\n+\t\t\treturn \"\"\n \t\t}\n+\t\treturn trimmed[:n]\n \t}\n \treturn \"\"\n }","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0186QbDjeRGLj2ZmiTCk611d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQzrw4YKttLBsxz1USft","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KC8wdatnX4TVTtSfqVfFGb","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-349050671/worktree/.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5502,"cache_read_input_tokens":43918,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5502},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"128382e2-987f-4117-b412-e29eb8d49ad3","timestamp":"2026-11-26T05:36:48.502Z","request_id":"req_011ChQzrvsPBjRjepzQGEDup","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KC8wdatnX4TVTtSfqVfFGb":{"file_path":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-349050671/worktree/.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KC8wdatnX4TVTtSfqVfFGb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tslug: pose-review-declared-threat-model\n3\tstatus: in-progress\n4\tcreated_at: 2026-11-23\n5\tcompleted_at:\n6\tsupersedes:\n7\tdepends_on: \n8\tremediates:\n9\tpriority: 4\n10\tcomponents: pose-mcp\n11\ttask_type: feature\n12\tsurface: standard\n13\tdelivers: capability:review-declared-threat-model\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t# Spec: pose-review-declared-threat-model\n17\t\n18\t## 1. Intent\n19\t\n20\t### Goal\n21\tLet a spec declare the accepted residual limits of a guarantee it makes, and let the review brief treat findings inside those limits as follow-ups to classify, not blockers to chase.\n22\t\n23\t### Business value\n24\tEach of the 23 rounds of `pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks` found a more remote adversarial variant: concurrent HEAD movement, case folding, Unicode normalization, symlinks inside submodules. Every one was real, but no round could converge, because the spec had no place to say which environment the guarantee assumes. A cap on rounds would approve something unreviewed; a declared threat model converges by explicit classification.\n25\t\n26\t### Constraints\n27\tA declared limit never hides a defect inside the declared scope. The reviewer may dispute a limit; the dispute is a finding with its own classification. Declared limits are visible in the closeout and the attestation.\n28\t\n29\t### Non-goals\n30\tLimiting the number of review rounds.\n31\t\n32\t## 2. Requirements\n33\t\n34\t### Functional\n35\t- R1: A spec shall be able to declare `### Accepted limits` entries with stable ids (`L<N>`), each naming the assumption and why it is accepted.\n36\t- R2: The review brief shall list the declared limits and instruct the reviewer to classify a finding that falls inside one as a follow-up naming the limit, or to dispute the limit explicitly.\n37\t- R3: The conclusion and the attestation shall record which findings were classified under which limit.\n38\t- R4: `lint-spec --ready-check` shall require accepted limits to be justified and the ADR, when present, to reference them.\n39\t\n40\t### Non-functional\n41\tExisting behaviour outside the stated requirements is unchanged.\n42\t\n43\t### Security\n44\tNo new input crosses a trust boundary without the existing confinement.\n45\t\n46\t### Compatibility\n47\tRecords and indexes written by earlier engines keep their meaning.\n48\t\n49\t## 3. Technical Plan\n50\t\n51\t### Affected areas\n52\t`pose-mcp` (CLI and engine), the manual and its embedded copy.\n53\t\n54\t### Artifacts\n55\t- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md\n56\t- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n57\t- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go\n58\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_plan.go\n59\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go\n60\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go\n61\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go\n62\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\n63\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n64\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_test.go\n65\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_versions_test.go\n66\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go\n67\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/testdata/review_brief_template_digests.json\n68\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n69\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go\n70\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/governance_stats.go\n71\t- modified: docs-site/docs/cli.md\n72\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes_test.go\n73\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_surface_test.go\n74\t- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/accepted_limits_lint_test.go\n75\t- modified: POSE.md\n76\t- modified: locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n77\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n78\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n79\t\n80\t\n81\t### Delivery targets\n82\t\n83\t- capability:review-declared-threat-model module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n84\t\n85\t\n86\t### Accepted limits\n87\t- L1: The limits are written by the implementer, so a limit can be broader than it should be. Accepted because the brief presents them as data to verify and a disputed limit is a blocking finding of its own.\n88\t- L2: A spec edited after sealing changes its limits only through a new bundle. Accepted because the limits enter the plan digest, so an edit stales the review as any other change does.\n89\t\n90\t### API/contract changes\n91\tA spec section `### Accepted limits` with `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` entries. The review plan and the bundle seal them as `accepted_limits` (omitted when none, so every other plan and bundle keeps its digest). A draft finding may carry `limit`; the attestation records it with disposition `within-limit`. Brief template `review-brief/11`. `lint-spec --ready-check` checks the section.\n92\t\n93\t\n94\t### Data/storage changes\n95\t`accepted_limits` in review plans and bundles and `limit` in draft and attestation findings, all omitted when empty.\n96\t\n97\t\n98\t### Technical risks\n99\tA limit could be used to wave a real defect through. It is the spec's own declaration, sealed into the plan digest and visible in the brief and the attestation; the reviewer may dispute it with a finding of its own, which then blocks as any finding does.\n100\t\n101\t\n102\t## 4. Tasks\n103\t\n104\t### Planning\n105\t- [ ] Confirm the requirements against the current engine before starting.\n106\t\n107\t### Implementation\n108\t- [ ] Implement each requirement with a test that fails before it.\n109\t\n110\t### Validation\n111\t- [ ] `pose validate --carry` and `pose check --strict`.\n112\t\n113\t## 5. Decisions\n114\t\n115\t### Decision D1\n116\t- Basis: R1, R2, R3\n117\tA finding inside a declared limit is neither an open finding nor an accepted risk: it gets its own disposition, `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity, requires a sealed limit and a reason, and records the limit's id. Accepted risks keep their severity bound; a limit is the spec's statement of scope, decided before the review.\n118\t\n119\t### Decision D2\n120\t- Basis: R1, R4\n121\tThe limits are sealed in the plan and the bundle, and in the plan digest, so the brief and the conclusion read them from what was reviewed, never from the current spec; both fields are omitted when empty so no earlier plan or bundle changes digest.\n122\t\n123\t## 6. Validation\n124\t\n125\t### Strategy\n126\tEach requirement gets a regression test shown failing before its code.\n127\t\n128\t### Deterministic checks\n129\t\n130\t#### Test\n131\t- Command: `go test ./...` (in `pose-mcp`), then `pose validate --carry`.\n132\t\n133\t### Execution log\n134\t- 2026-11-25: implemented R1–R4: `ParseAcceptedLimits`, the plan and bundle `accepted_limits`, the draft `limit` and the `within-limit` disposition, `review-brief/11` and the ready check. Each has a test that fails with it mutated out; the first digest test survived because the spec edit changed the scope digest too, and now compares the plan's digest with and without the limits.\n135\t- 2026-11-25: review round 1 (rrn-d1680df442b23d12) requested changes. N1: in a scope with several specs (a milestone or a roadmap) each limit id is qualified by its spec as `<spec>#L<N>`, with the spec recorded, so a finding names exactly one sealed limit. N2: the reason must follow `Accepted because`, not only the marker. N3: an ADR names a limit only by the id as a word, so L10 does not name L1. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n136\t- 2026-11-25: review round 2 (rrn-256db7e6eea5a6e2) found that punctuation after the marker passed as a reason and a one-letter first word failed. The reason must now hold at least two words with letters (`hasAcceptedLimitReason`), with a test that fails with the rule mutated out.\n137\t- 2026-11-25: review round 3 (rrn-0af0d413761142d8) found that the marker matched inside a sentence, so 'Not accepted because ...' passed. The marker must now open a sentence (start of the text or after `.`, `;`, `:`, `!` or `?`), as the format `<assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` states; the test fails with the boundary mutated out.\n138\t- 2026-11-25: review round 4 (rrn-721e2a4c9bd37e2e) found that an entry with no assumption, or with an empty reason followed by other prose, passed. The entry is now read structurally: an assumption of at least two words, the marker opening a sentence, and a reason of at least two words within that same sentence. Each rule has a test case that fails with it mutated out.\n139\t- 2026-11-25: review round 5 (rrn-9f6867b3c3e42377): N1, a whitespace-only reason of a `within-limit` finding is refused; N2, the ready check fails closed on an ADR directory or file it cannot read, keeping the absent-directory case. Both have tests that fail with them mutated out.\n140\t- 2026-11-25: review round 6 (rrn-a5b61489ac04873d) found the bundle's plan digest did not cover the sealed limits; it now does, omitted when empty so every other bundle keeps its digest. `TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits` checks both and fails with the field mutated out.\n141\t- 2026-11-25: review round 7 (rrn-1236e104ff346a2d): N2, the ready check reads an ADR only as a regular file, without following a link and within 1 MiB, so a link to an endless source fails instead of hanging; the test fails with the bounded read mutated out. N1 (low) was classified by the reviewer within L1: the check is a format gate, and the substance of a limit is judged in review.\n142\t- 2026-11-26: round 8 hit the Codex usage limit; by the maintainer's decision Claude Sonnet 5.5 reviews from here (a `claude-sonnet-5-5` profile in the delegation policy, the bundle resealed with the declared lead `anthropic/claude-opus-5-5` so the review is `different-model`; vendor independence is not achieved). Its round (rrn-b874a0e13bc62075) recorded N6 within L1, the first live use of `limit`, and requested changes: N1, a wrapped entry now keeps its continuation lines; N2, fenced blocks and HTML comments are not read as declarations; N3, `review-brief/11` (not yet published, so its digest was re-recorded) states the exception to rule (i) and lists `limit` among the finding fields; N4, an ADR cites the spec only by its slug as a whole name; N5, governance outcomes count `within-limit` findings in their own bucket. Each has a test that fails with it mutated out. `gofmt` over the package's tests also reformatted `delivery_surface_test.go` (formatting only), declared among the artifacts.\n143\t- 2026-11-26: review round 9 (rrn-c3e24e7943c0693e, Claude Sonnet 5.5) requested changes. N1: the claim on `.pose/policy/delegation.json` is withdrawn: the reviewer profile is an operational change of this checkout, committed without the trailer, not a deliverable of the spec. N2: an entry is one Markdown list item: indented lines (also after a blank line) continue it, an unindented line or the next `-`/`*` item ends it, a wrapped `#` is no heading, and a nested list or a limit without its reason is reported and not sealed. N3: an ADR that links the spec's dated file cites it. N4 (fences close only with their own marker) and N5 (the governance text prints `within_limit`) are fixed in part; the rest are follow-ups. N6: the conclusion step of `review-brief/11` (re-recorded digest) says a follow-up with a sealed limit is recorded within it. N7 was recorded within L1. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n144\t- 2026-11-26: review round 10 (rrn-87881c122439e5f3) requested changes: a lazy unindented continuation and a fence inside an item still cut a limit. The section is now read fail-closed: it holds only limits, each one list item (`-`, `*` or `+`, indented up to three spaces) whose wrapped lines are indented under its marker; any other line (prose, a lazy continuation, a nested list, a fence, an ordered item) is reported, and the limit it belongs to is not sealed, so a limit is never sealed in part. Inline triple backticks no longer open a fence, an indented heading is read, an unterminated HTML comment is reported (N2, N4 in part), and the CLI reference states `within-limit` (N3). Each rule has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n145\t\n146\t\n147\t### Results summary\n148\tThe full `pose-mcp` suite passes; the canonical result is refreshed with `pose validate --carry`.\n149\t\n150\t\n151\t### Requirement trace\n152\t- R1 [satisfied] test:TestAcceptedLimitsAreParsed, test:TestAcceptedLimitsReadWrappedEntriesAndSkipExamples, test:TestTheReviewPlanSealsTheAcceptedLimits, test:TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits, test:TestLimitsAcrossSpecsAreQualified, test:TestAnAcceptedLimitNeedsWordsAsItsReason\n153\t- R2 [satisfied] test:TestTheCurrentBriefListsTheAcceptedLimits\n154\t- R3 [satisfied] test:TestAFindingWithinASealedLimitDoesNotBlockApproval, test:TestGovernanceOutcomesCountsJudgmentAndInvalidArtifacts\n155\t- R4 [satisfied] test:TestReadyCheckJudgesAcceptedLimits\n156\t\n157\t\n158\t### Known gaps\n159\tNone.\n160\t\n161\t\n162\t## 7. Final Report\n163\t\n164\t### Delivered scope\n165\tSpecs declare accepted limits; the review plan and bundle seal them; `review-brief/11` lists them and tells the reviewer to classify a finding inside one with `limit`; the attestation records it `within-limit`, which does not block an approval; `lint-spec --ready-check` requires each limit to say why it is accepted and an ADR citing the spec to name it.\n166\t\n167\t\n168\t### Files and modules changed\n169\t`pose-mcp` (plan, bundle, conclusion, brief, lint), the manual in both locales and its embedded copies.\n170\t\n171\t\n172\t### Validation executed\n173\t`go test ./...` in `pose-mcp`; `pose check --strict`; `pose validate --carry --json-out .pose/results/delivery-validation.json`.\n174\t\n175\t\n176\t### Residual risks\n177\tL1 and L2 above.\n178\t\n179\t\n180\t### Follow-ups\n181\t- [open] Keep an HTML comment written inside inline code in an accepted limit's sealed text, which the comment stripping now removes (review rounds 9 N4 and 10 N4). (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-12-26)\n182\t- [open] Show within-limit findings in the text output of `pose review-check` and `pose closeout-check`, as the JSON already does (review round 9, N5). (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-12-26)\n183\t\n184\t\n185\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"2f5f3638-db46-44f3-904c-d0146d31a874","timestamp":"2026-11-26T05:36:48.511Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-349050671/worktree/.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md","content":"---\nslug: pose-review-declared-threat-model\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-23\ncompleted_at:\nsupersedes:\ndepends_on: \nremediates:\npriority: 4\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: feature\nsurface: standard\ndelivers: capability:review-declared-threat-model\n---\n\n# Spec: pose-review-declared-threat-model\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\nLet a spec declare the accepted residual limits of a guarantee it makes, and let the review brief treat findings inside those limits as follow-ups to classify, not blockers to chase.\n\n### Business value\nEach of the 23 rounds of `pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks` found a more remote adversarial variant: concurrent HEAD movement, case folding, Unicode normalization, symlinks inside submodules. Every one was real, but no round could converge, because the spec had no place to say which environment the guarantee assumes. A cap on rounds would approve something unreviewed; a declared threat model converges by explicit classification.\n\n### Constraints\nA declared limit never hides a defect inside the declared scope. The reviewer may dispute a limit; the dispute is a finding with its own classification. Declared limits are visible in the closeout and the attestation.\n\n### Non-goals\nLimiting the number of review rounds.\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- R1: A spec shall be able to declare `### Accepted limits` entries with stable ids (`L<N>`), each naming the assumption and why it is accepted.\n- R2: The review brief shall list the declared limits and instruct the reviewer to classify a finding that falls inside one as a follow-up naming the limit, or to dispute the limit explicitly.\n- R3: The conclusion and the attestation shall record which findings were classified under which limit.\n- R4: `lint-spec --ready-check` shall require accepted limits to be justified and the ADR, when present, to reference them.\n\n### Non-functional\nExisting behaviour outside the stated requirements is unchanged.\n\n### Security\nNo new input crosses a trust boundary without the existing confinement.\n\n### Compatibility\nRecords and indexes written by earlier engines keep their meaning.\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n`pose-mcp` (CLI and engine), the manual and its embedded copy.\n\n### Artifacts\n- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_plan.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_versions_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/testdata/review_brief_template_digests.json\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/governance_stats.go\n- modified: docs-site/docs/cli.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/governance_outcomes_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_surface_test.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/accepted_limits_lint_test.go\n- modified: POSE.md\n- modified: locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n- capability:review-declared-threat-model module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go\n\n\n### Accepted limits\n- L1: The limits are written by the implementer, so a limit can be broader than it should be. Accepted because the brief presents them as data to verify and a disputed limit is a blocking finding of its own.\n- L2: A spec edited after sealing changes its limits only through a new bundle. Accepted because the limits enter the plan digest, so an edit stales the review as any other change does.\n\n### API/contract changes\nA spec section `### Accepted limits` with `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` entries. The review plan and the bundle seal them as `accepted_limits` (omitted when none, so every other plan and bundle keeps its digest). A draft finding may carry `limit`; the attestation records it with disposition `within-limit`. Brief template `review-brief/11`. `lint-spec --ready-check` checks the section.\n\n\n### Data/storage changes\n`accepted_limits` in review plans and bundles and `limit` in draft and attestation findings, all omitted when empty.\n\n\n### Technical risks\nA limit could be used to wave a real defect through. It is the spec's own declaration, sealed into the plan digest and visible in the brief and the attestation; the reviewer may dispute it with a finding of its own, which then blocks as any finding does.\n\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n### Planning\n- [ ] Confirm the requirements against the current engine before starting.\n\n### Implementation\n- [ ] Implement each requirement with a test that fails before it.\n\n### Validation\n- [ ] `pose validate --carry` and `pose check --strict`.\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\n- Basis: R1, R2, R3\nA finding inside a declared limit is neither an open finding nor an accepted risk: it gets its own disposition, `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity, requires a sealed limit and a reason, and records the limit's id. Accepted risks keep their severity bound; a limit is the spec's statement of scope, decided before the review.\n\n### Decision D2\n- Basis: R1, R4\nThe limits are sealed in the plan and the bundle, and in the plan digest, so the brief and the conclusion read them from what was reviewed, never from the current spec; both fields are omitted when empty so no earlier plan or bundle changes digest.\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\nEach requirement gets a regression test shown failing before its code.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n#### Test\n- Command: `go test ./...` (in `pose-mcp`), then `pose validate --carry`.\n\n### Execution log\n- 2026-11-25: implemented R1–R4: `ParseAcceptedLimits`, the plan and bundle `accepted_limits`, the draft `limit` and the `within-limit` disposition, `review-brief/11` and the ready check. Each has a test that fails with it mutated out; the first digest test survived because the spec edit changed the scope digest too, and now compares the plan's digest with and without the limits.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 1 (rrn-d1680df442b23d12) requested changes. N1: in a scope with several specs (a milestone or a roadmap) each limit id is qualified by its spec as `<spec>#L<N>`, with the spec recorded, so a finding names exactly one sealed limit. N2: the reason must follow `Accepted because`, not only the marker. N3: an ADR names a limit only by the id as a word, so L10 does not name L1. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 2 (rrn-256db7e6eea5a6e2) found that punctuation after the marker passed as a reason and a one-letter first word failed. The reason must now hold at least two words with letters (`hasAcceptedLimitReason`), with a test that fails with the rule mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 3 (rrn-0af0d413761142d8) found that the marker matched inside a sentence, so 'Not accepted because ...' passed. The marker must now open a sentence (start of the text or after `.`, `;`, `:`, `!` or `?`), as the format `<assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` states; the test fails with the boundary mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 4 (rrn-721e2a4c9bd37e2e) found that an entry with no assumption, or with an empty reason followed by other prose, passed. The entry is now read structurally: an assumption of at least two words, the marker opening a sentence, and a reason of at least two words within that same sentence. Each rule has a test case that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 5 (rrn-9f6867b3c3e42377): N1, a whitespace-only reason of a `within-limit` finding is refused; N2, the ready check fails closed on an ADR directory or file it cannot read, keeping the absent-directory case. Both have tests that fail with them mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 6 (rrn-a5b61489ac04873d) found the bundle's plan digest did not cover the sealed limits; it now does, omitted when empty so every other bundle keeps its digest. `TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits` checks both and fails with the field mutated out.\n- 2026-11-25: review round 7 (rrn-1236e104ff346a2d): N2, the ready check reads an ADR only as a regular file, without following a link and within 1 MiB, so a link to an endless source fails instead of hanging; the test fails with the bounded read mutated out. N1 (low) was classified by the reviewer within L1: the check is a format gate, and the substance of a limit is judged in review.\n- 2026-11-26: round 8 hit the Codex usage limit; by the maintainer's decision Claude Sonnet 5.5 reviews from here (a `claude-sonnet-5-5` profile in the delegation policy, the bundle resealed with the declared lead `anthropic/claude-opus-5-5` so the review is `different-model`; vendor independence is not achieved). Its round (rrn-b874a0e13bc62075) recorded N6 within L1, the first live use of `limit`, and requested changes: N1, a wrapped entry now keeps its continuation lines; N2, fenced blocks and HTML comments are not read as declarations; N3, `review-brief/11` (not yet published, so its digest was re-recorded) states the exception to rule (i) and lists `limit` among the finding fields; N4, an ADR cites the spec only by its slug as a whole name; N5, governance outcomes count `within-limit` findings in their own bucket. Each has a test that fails with it mutated out. `gofmt` over the package's tests also reformatted `delivery_surface_test.go` (formatting only), declared among the artifacts.\n- 2026-11-26: review round 9 (rrn-c3e24e7943c0693e, Claude Sonnet 5.5) requested changes. N1: the claim on `.pose/policy/delegation.json` is withdrawn: the reviewer profile is an operational change of this checkout, committed without the trailer, not a deliverable of the spec. N2: an entry is one Markdown list item: indented lines (also after a blank line) continue it, an unindented line or the next `-`/`*` item ends it, a wrapped `#` is no heading, and a nested list or a limit without its reason is reported and not sealed. N3: an ADR that links the spec's dated file cites it. N4 (fences close only with their own marker) and N5 (the governance text prints `within_limit`) are fixed in part; the rest are follow-ups. N6: the conclusion step of `review-brief/11` (re-recorded digest) says a follow-up with a sealed limit is recorded within it. N7 was recorded within L1. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-26: review round 10 (rrn-87881c122439e5f3) requested changes: a lazy unindented continuation and a fence inside an item still cut a limit. The section is now read fail-closed: it holds only limits, each one list item (`-`, `*` or `+`, indented up to three spaces) whose wrapped lines are indented under its marker; any other line (prose, a lazy continuation, a nested list, a fence, an ordered item) is reported, and the limit it belongs to is not sealed, so a limit is never sealed in part. Inline triple backticks no longer open a fence, an indented heading is read, an unterminated HTML comment is reported (N2, N4 in part), and the CLI reference states `within-limit` (N3). Each rule has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n\n\n### Results summary\nThe full `pose-mcp` suite passes; the canonical result is refreshed with `pose validate --carry`.\n\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestAcceptedLimitsAreParsed, test:TestAcceptedLimitsReadWrappedEntriesAndSkipExamples, test:TestTheReviewPlanSealsTheAcceptedLimits, test:TestTheBundlePlanDigestCoversTheAcceptedLimits, test:TestLimitsAcrossSpecsAreQualified, test:TestAnAcceptedLimitNeedsWordsAsItsReason\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestTheCurrentBriefListsTheAcceptedLimits\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestAFindingWithinASealedLimitDoesNotBlockApproval, test:TestGovernanceOutcomesCountsJudgmentAndInvalidArtifacts\n- R4 [satisfied] test:TestReadyCheckJudgesAcceptedLimits\n\n\n### Known gaps\nNone.\n\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\nSpecs declare accepted limits; the review plan and bundle seal them; `review-brief/11` lists them and tells the reviewer to classify a finding inside one with `limit`; the attestation records it `within-limit`, which does not block an approval; `lint-spec --ready-check` requires each limit to say why it is accepted and an ADR citing the spec to name it.\n\n\n### Files and modules changed\n`pose-mcp` (plan, bundle, conclusion, brief, lint), the manual in both locales and its embedded copies.\n\n\n### Validation executed\n`go test ./...` in `pose-mcp`; `pose check --strict`; `pose validate --carry --json-out .pose/results/delivery-validation.json`.\n\n\n### Residual risks\nL1 and L2 above.\n\n\n### Follow-ups\n- [open] Keep an HTML comment written inside inline code in an accepted limit's sealed text, which the comment stripping now removes (review rounds 9 N4 and 10 N4). (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-12-26)\n- [open] Show within-limit findings in the text output of `pose review-check` and `pose closeout-check`, as the JSON already does (review round 9, N5). (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-12-26)\n\n\n","numLines":185,"startLine":1,"totalLines":185}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KC8wdatnX4TVTtSfqVfFGb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQzrw4YKttLBsxz1USft","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Fp7TKo278qW5mRJfUMw9U6","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-349050671/worktree/pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go","offset":440,"limit":240},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5502,"cache_read_input_tokens":43918,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5502},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"2fbab11b-e5f0-4995-bbe6-05a479fb7ae2","timestamp":"2026-11-26T05:36:49.183Z","request_id":"req_011ChQzrvsPBjRjepzQGEDup","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Fp7TKo278qW5mRJfUMw9U6":{"file_path":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-349050671/worktree/pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go","offset":440,"limit":240}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fp7TKo278qW5mRJfUMw9U6","type":"tool_result","content":"440\t// collide in the document's field map, and every finding already names its spec.\n441\tfunc lintOneSpecWith(r *cliout.Renderer, fields bool, specPath string, requiredOnly, readyCheck bool, stderr io.Writer, checkDesign bool) int {\n442\t\tfield := func(name, value string) {\n443\t\t\tif fields {\n444\t\t\t\tr.Field(name, value)\n445\t\t\t}\n446\t\t}\n447\t\tlocale := cliLocaleValue()\n448\t\traw, err := os.ReadFile(specPath)\n449\t\tif err != nil {\n450\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: spec not found: %s\\n\", \"Erro: spec ausente: %s\\n\"), specPath)\n451\t\t\treturn 2\n452\t\t}\n453\t\tfrontmatter := lintParseFrontmatter(string(raw))\n454\t\ttext := htmlCommentRE.ReplaceAllString(string(raw), \"\")\n455\t\tsections := splitLintSections(text)\n456\t\tslug := frontmatter[\"slug\"]\n457\t\tif slug == \"\" {\n458\t\t\tslug = filepath.Base(filepath.Dir(specPath))\n459\t\t}\n460\t\tlint := specFindings{r: r, slug: slug}\n461\t\tif frontmatter[\"status\"] == \"superseded\" {\n462\t\t\tif canonical, ok := posepkg.VerifiedTransferStub(lintProjectRoot(specPath), slug, string(raw)); ok {\n463\t\t\t\t// A verified redirect owns no requirements or lifecycle of its\n464\t\t\t\t// own; the canonical task is linted where it lives.\n465\t\t\t\tfield(\"spec.redirect\", canonical.String())\n466\t\t\t\treturn 0\n467\t\t\t}\n468\t\t}\n469\t\tlineageFailures := 0\n470\t\tif links := posepkg.RemediationValues(frontmatter[\"remediates\"]); len(links) > 0 {\n471\t\t\troot, lineageErr := projectRootAt(filepath.Dir(specPath))\n472\t\t\tif lineageErr == nil {\n473\t\t\t\tlineageErr = (posepkg.Store{Root: root}).ValidateRemediationLineage(posepkg.Spec{Slug: slug, Remediates: links})\n474\t\t\t}\n475\t\t\tif lineageErr != nil {\n476\t\t\t\tlint.finding(cliout.StateError, \"remediation-lineage\", lineageErr.Error())\n477\t\t\t\tlineageFailures++\n478\t\t\t}\n479\t\t\tfield(\"spec.remediation.links\", strconv.Itoa(len(links)))\n480\t\t\tfield(\"spec.remediation.failures\", strconv.Itoa(lineageFailures))\n481\t\t}\n482\t\tdesignFailures := 0\n483\t\temitDesignBasis := func() {\n484\t\t\tif !checkDesign {\n485\t\t\t\treturn\n486\t\t\t}\n487\t\t\troot, rootErr := projectRootAt(filepath.Dir(specPath))\n488\t\t\tif rootErr != nil {\n489\t\t\t\troot = \"\"\n490\t\t\t}\n491\t\t\treport := posepkg.ValidateDesignBasis(string(raw), root)\n492\t\t\t// Buffered so the fields follow the findings, as they always have.\n493\t\t\tdesignFields := [][2]string{}\n494\t\t\tdesignFields = append(designFields, [2]string{\"spec.design_basis.present\", strconv.FormatBool(report.HasSection)})\n495\t\t\tdesignFields = append(designFields, [2]string{\"spec.design_basis.assumptions\", strconv.Itoa(len(report.Assumptions))})\n496\t\t\tdesignFields = append(designFields, [2]string{\"spec.design_basis.decisions\", strconv.Itoa(len(report.Decisions))})\n497\t\t\tdesignFields = append(designFields, [2]string{\"spec.design_basis.diagnostics\", strconv.Itoa(len(report.Diagnostics))})\n498\t\t\terrors, warnings := 0, 0\n499\t\t\tfor _, diagnostic := range report.Diagnostics {\n500\t\t\t\tstate := cliout.StateWarning\n501\t\t\t\tif diagnostic.Severity == \"error\" {\n502\t\t\t\t\tstate = cliout.StateError\n503\t\t\t\t\terrors++\n504\t\t\t\t} else {\n505\t\t\t\t\twarnings++\n506\t\t\t\t}\n507\t\t\t\tmessage := fmt.Sprintf(\"design basis line %d: %s\", diagnostic.Line, diagnostic.Message)\n508\t\t\t\tlint.finding(state, \"design-basis/\"+diagnostic.Code, message)\n509\t\t\t}\n510\t\t\tdesignFailures = errors\n511\t\t\tdesignFields = append(designFields, [2]string{\"spec.design_basis.errors\", strconv.Itoa(errors)})\n512\t\t\tdesignFields = append(designFields, [2]string{\"spec.design_basis.warnings\", strconv.Itoa(warnings)})\n513\t\t\tif report.Digest != \"\" {\n514\t\t\t\tdesignFields = append(designFields, [2]string{\"spec.design_basis.digest\", report.Digest})\n515\t\t\t}\n516\t\t\tfor _, f := range designFields {\n517\t\t\t\tfield(f[0], f[1])\n518\t\t\t}\n519\t\t}\n520\t\n521\t\t// The verdicts delegated adjudications recorded on this spec (spec\n522\t\t// pose-delegated-verdict-destinations R1): a question the current bundle's\n523\t\t// adjudication says does not hold fails the lint; one it cannot determine,\n524\t\t// and a record that does not verify, are warned about.\n525\t\tadjudicationFailures := 0\n526\t\tif root, err := projectRootAt(filepath.Dir(specPath)); err == nil {\n527\t\t\tif adjudications, err := (posepkg.Store{Root: root}).SpecAdjudications(slug); err == nil && len(adjudications) > 0 {\n528\t\t\t\tfor _, a := range adjudications {\n529\t\t\t\t\tswitch {\n530\t\t\t\t\tcase !a.Verified:\n531\t\t\t\t\t\tlint.finding(cliout.StateWarning, \"adjudication\", fmt.Sprintf(cliText(locale, \"%s does not verify: %s\", \"%s não verifica: %s\"), a.Path, a.Why))\n532\t\t\t\t\tcase !a.Current:\n533\t\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n534\t\t\t\t\tdefault:\n535\t\t\t\t\t\tfor _, q := range a.Record.Questions {\n536\t\t\t\t\t\t\tswitch q.Verdict {\n537\t\t\t\t\t\t\tcase posepkg.AdjudicationDoesNotHold:\n538\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tlint.finding(cliout.StateError, \"adjudication\", fmt.Sprintf(cliText(locale, \"%s does not hold, adjudicated by %s: %s\", \"%s não se sustenta, adjudicado por %s: %s\"), q.ID, a.Record.RunID, q.Rationale))\n539\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tadjudicationFailures++\n540\t\t\t\t\t\t\tcase posepkg.AdjudicationCannotDetermine:\n541\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tlint.finding(cliout.StateWarning, \"adjudication\", fmt.Sprintf(cliText(locale, \"%s cannot be determined, adjudicated by %s: %s\", \"%s não pôde ser determinado, adjudicado por %s: %s\"), q.ID, a.Record.RunID, q.Rationale))\n542\t\t\t\t\t\t\t}\n543\t\t\t\t\t\t}\n544\t\t\t\t\t}\n545\t\t\t\t}\n546\t\t\t\tfield(\"spec.adjudications\", strconv.Itoa(len(adjudications)))\n547\t\t\t\tfield(\"spec.adjudications.failures\", strconv.Itoa(adjudicationFailures))\n548\t\t\t}\n549\t\t}\n550\t\tif readyCheck {\n551\t\t\tfailures := 0\n552\t\t\tfor _, name := range []string{\"Intent\", \"Requirements\", \"Technical Plan\"} {\n553\t\t\t\tlines, ok := sections[name]\n554\t\t\t\tif !ok || classifySection(lines) != \"filled\" {\n555\t\t\t\t\tlint.finding(cliout.StateError, \"dor\", fmt.Sprintf(cliText(locale, \"DoR: section %s is missing, empty, or skeletal\", \"DoR: seção %s ausente/vazia/esquelética\"), name))\n556\t\t\t\t\tfailures++\n557\t\t\t\t}\n558\t\t\t}\n559\t\t\tif len(parseRequirementIDs(sections[\"Requirements\"])) == 0 {\n560\t\t\t\tlint.finding(cliout.StateError, \"dor\", cliText(locale, \"DoR: no acceptance criterion has a stable ID (use '- R<N>: ...' bullets in Requirements)\", \"DoR: nenhum acceptance criterion com ID estável (use bullets '- R<N>: ...' em Requirements)\"))\n561\t\t\t\tfailures++\n562\t\t\t}\n563\t\t\tfor _, ref := range lintParseDependsOn(frontmatter[\"depends_on\"]) {\n564\t\t\t\tif _, err := posepkg.ParseArtifactRef(ref); err == nil {\n565\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n566\t\t\t\t}\n567\t\t\t\tlint.finding(cliout.StateError, \"dor\", fmt.Sprintf(cliText(locale, \"DoR: invalid depends_on reference: '%s'\", \"DoR: ref inválida em depends_on: '%s'\"), ref))\n568\t\t\t\tfailures++\n569\t\t\t}\n570\t\t\t// Accepted limits (spec pose-review-declared-threat-model R4): each\n571\t\t\t// says why it is accepted, and an ADR that names the spec names each\n572\t\t\t// limit, so the decision record carries what the review will treat as\n573\t\t\t// out of scope.\n574\t\t\tlimits, limitProblems := posepkg.ParseAcceptedLimits(text)\n575\t\t\tfor _, problem := range limitProblems {\n576\t\t\t\tlint.finding(cliout.StateError, \"dor\", \"DoR: \"+problem)\n577\t\t\t\tfailures++\n578\t\t\t}\n579\t\t\tif len(limits) > 0 {\n580\t\t\t\tfor _, missing := range acceptedLimitsMissingFromADRs(lintProjectRoot(specPath), slug, limits) {\n581\t\t\t\t\tlint.finding(cliout.StateError, \"dor\", missing)\n582\t\t\t\t\tfailures++\n583\t\t\t\t}\n584\t\t\t}\n58